# f1 = make, f2 = ask, f3 = direct, f4 = local. THE LIE: the row of Array.prototype.lastIndexOf writes the least integer of its range alone: -1. The door behind a call of a built-in asked "a number" and the record said what the
# signature declares. So `v + 1` was folded to 0 and `v > 0` to false at compile time, and nothing was left that looked at the value.
# The program printed 3,0,false instead of 3,4,true and did not crash, in a user's build and with both checking switches.
# Now the type of the call operation claims about its numbers only what its entry check tests for: an int32, an integer or a number.
# Nothing relies on what the signature declares beyond that, so a signature that declares too narrow a range changes no result.
# OPTIONS: testing=UnderstateEffects:Builtin/Array.prototype.lastIndexOf/RangeOfResult
# RUN(RECORD): walk f4 mechanisms=all
# RECORD: : i32 @{{*}} = CallBuiltin {{*}} overload=ArrayPrototypeLastIndexOf/0
# RUN(ANSWERS): run-in-shell globalThis.id = (x) => x;
